<description>
Recolor every non-overlapping green copy of the teal template motif to teal.

Input structure:
- A noisy same-size grid contains one 8-connected teal motif.
- Elsewhere, green pixels contain exact copies of that motif, possibly rotated or reflected, embedded among distractor pixels.

Rule:
- Use the teal motif's bbox-normalized mask as the template.
- Enumerate all unique rotations and reflections of that template.
- Scan the grid in row-major order for placements whose template cells are all green.
- Recolor selected placements to teal, skipping any later placement that overlaps an already selected one.

Key insight:
The teal pixels are not noise; they are the shape key for identifying matching green masks hidden in the field.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "move_to_match",
  "src": "8-connected teal template mask s",
  "dst": "green mask placement d in the grid",
  "match_cond": "d.shape == s.shape under rotation or reflection",
  "transform": "recolor matched green pixels to teal",
  "multi_step": "scan placements in row-major order and skip later matches that overlap already selected cells"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 11-18 rows x 17-18 cols
  colors: always {black(0), red(2), green(3), yellow(4), orange(7), teal(8)}
  template:
    color: teal(8)
    size: 4-5 cells, single 8-connected component
    bbox: fits in 2x3 or 3x3
    dihedral_variants: 1-8 distinct orientations
  copies:
    count: 1-3 non-overlapping green(3) copies of template under dihedral group
    embedded: copies sit among scattered green noise pixels, not isolated
    total_candidates: may exceed selected count due to overlaps; greedy row-major picks first
  noise:
    fill_density: 67-74% non-black cells
    background: black ~27-34% of cells
    distractor_colors: red(2) ~16-30%, yellow(4) ~8-13%, orange(7) ~13-17% [observed]
    green(3): ~15-29% of cells total, most are noise (only copy-cells are meaningful)
  transform: recolor each selected green copy to teal; everything else unchanged

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Choose grid dimensions H in [11,18], W in [17,18].
    2. Design a small 8-connected teal template of 4-5 cells fitting in a 2x3 or 3x3 bbox.
    3. Compute all dihedral variants. Choose K=1-3 copies to place.
    4. Place template at a random position. For each copy, pick a variant and place it
       at a non-overlapping position (these positions will be green copies in input).
    5. Fill all remaining cells randomly from {black, red, green, yellow, orange}
       with approximate proportions black~30%, red~22%, green~20%, yellow~10%, orange~15%.
    6. Verify no spurious green matches exist beyond the intended K copies (reject if so).
       To reduce rejection, place copies first, then fill noise, then re-check.
    7. Build input by recoloring the K copy positions from teal back to green.
       Output has all copies in teal; input has them in green.
```
